From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: model the video PLLs and the eDP/TV pixel clock

This tree has never modelled a video PLL. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` added the eDP *bus* gate and
stated in its own header why it stopped there: "Its mux cannot be expressed by
this tree yet. CLK_SRC_SEL [26:24] selects 000 VIDEO0PLL4X, 001 VIDEO1PLL4X,
010 VIDEO2PLL4X, 011 PERI0PLL2X, and this CCU models none of the three video
PLLs. [...] Modelling a four-way mux with three parents missing would be a
guess, and guesses do not ship here." This patch is the answer to that
sentence: all four sources, the pixel divider, and the two VIDEO_OUT1 bus
gates the pixel path needs.

    pll-ref 24 MHz -> PLL_VIDEOx (VCO) -> VIDEOxPLL4X -> EDP_TV_CLK -> TCON_TV1

New clocks: pll-video0/1/2, video0/1/2-pll4x, edp-tv, bus-tcon-tv1,
bus-dpss-top1. New resets: RST_BUS_TCON_TV1, RST_BUS_DPSS_TOP1. Nothing
references them until `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`'s device tree does, so on its own this patch changes
no register except the two probe-time bits named below.

The collision is concrete, not argued. This patch edits three files `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset`
already edits, and in all three the collision is in trailing context:

  include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-ccu.h   `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset`'s hunk ends on
      CLK_BUS_PWM1 / blank / #endif; ids appended before #endif earlier in the series move
      that context and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` no longer applies at fuzz 0.
  include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h   same shape.
  drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c           `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` changes
      ".num = CLK_BUS_PWM1 + 1" to ".num = CLK_BUS_EDP + 1"; this patch changes
      the same line again. Whichever applies second has to see what the first
      one left.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
Register geometry: the CCU field tables of the A733 user manual (extracted from
A733 UM V0.92 4.1.6), cross-read against the UM text, with
the CCU base 0x02002000 from the memory map of the A733 user manual.

  PLL_VIDEO0/1/2_CTRL_REG  0x0120 / 0x0140 / 0x0160
    reset 0x40126200, one layout for all three
    31 PLL_EN . 30 PLL_LDO_EN . 29 LOCK_ENABLE . 28 LOCK (RO)
    27 PLL_OUTPUT0_GATE ("4X") . 26 PLL_OUTPUT1_GATE ("3X")
    [22:20] PLL_OUTPUT0_DIV  P0 = field+1, 0..7
    [18:16] PLL_OUTPUT1_DIV  P1 = field+1, 0..7
    [15:8]  PLL_N            N  = field+1, field>=11
    [1]     PLL_INPUT_DIV    M1 = field+1, 0..1
    VIDEOxPLL4X = InputFreq * N / M1 / P0
    VIDEOxPLL3X = InputFreq * N / M1 / P1
    VCO 1.26G..2.52G, P0/P1 1..8   UM Table 4-2
  EDP_TV_CLK_REG           0x1640
    31 gate . [26:24] CLK_SRC_SEL . [12:8] FACTOR_N . [4:0] FACTOR_M
    EDP_TV_CLK = Clock Source / M / N
  TCONTV1_BGR_REG          0x160C  bit0 gate, bit16 reset
  DPSS_TOP1_BGR_REG        0x16CC  bit0 gate, bit16 reset

Silicon, three reads, none of them from code:
  - the 8 GiB board at the U-Boot prompt, 2026-08-19: PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL = 0x41126200 --
    PLL off, every configurable field at UM Table 4-3's documented default
    (N 99, M1 1, P0 2, P1 3, VCO 2376), and all four display gate registers 0.
  - the 6 GiB board under the vendor stack driving a live eDP link, 2026-07-18:
    PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL = 0xfd126200 (the one live video PLL, VIDEO0/2 off),
    EDP_TV_CLK = 0x81000000 -> CLK_SRC_SEL 001 = VIDEO1PLL4X.
  - the 8 GiB board on our own 6.18.36 driving a picture by hand, 2026-08-18:
    PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL = 0xFD524C00, EDP_TV_CLK = 0x81000001, TCONTV1_BGR and
    DPSS_TOP1_BGR both 0x00010001.

THE DECISION THIS PATCH HAD TO MAKE: REAL set_rate, OR ONE CONSTANT
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Whether PLL_VIDEO1 should be a real clk_ops with set_rate for an arbitrary
mode, rather than the one constant 1920x1200 needs, was genuinely uncertain,
and this PLL has never been asked for a second mode.

Real clk_ops, and rate propagation ON. The argument, in order:

 1. A constant would describe hardware that does not exist. N, M1 and both post
    dividers are R/W fields with documented ranges; a fixed-rate model would
    force a display driver to poke CCU registers behind the clock framework,
    which is the workaround shape this tree refuses.
 2. Propagation is what makes it usable: one clk_set_rate() on the pixel clock
    configures PLL, tap and divider, which is the idiom every mainline sunxi
    display driver uses (clk_set_rate(sclk, mode->crtc_clock * 1000)).
 3. It is the OPPOSITE of what the SMHC block in this same file does, and that
    is deliberate. The SMHC comment records a measured bug: CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
    onto FIXED taps of a shared peripheral PLL, so the request could not be
    honoured, the framework recorded a parent rate it never programmed, and an
    eMMC bus ran at 800 MHz while sysfs said 200 (2026-07-25). Here every stage
    is genuinely settable and owned by one consumer, so the request IS
    honoured. Same flag, opposite conclusion, and the difference is the reason.
 4. The claim is checkable rather than promised. Walking the three mainline
    routines by hand for the one mode that has run on silicon -- 154 MHz --
    ccu_mp_find_best_with_parent_adj() tries m*p = 2, asks the tap for 308 MHz;
    clk_divider_bestdiv() tries div 6, asks the PLL for 1848 MHz;
    ccu_nm_find_best() returns N = 77, M1 = 1 exactly (24 * 77 = 1848, and n
    ascending makes 77/1 win over the equally exact 154/2). Fields programmed:

      PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL  [15:8] = 0x4C, [1] = 0, [22:20] = 5
      EDP_TV_CLK       [12:8] = 0,    [4:0] = 1

    which is field for field what was written by hand for the picture of
    2026-08-18. For 154 MHz VCO 1848 is the unique solution anyway -- gcd(77,12) = 1
    forces the combined divisor to 12 -- so there was no other landing site.
 5. What it owes, stated as one observable: a SECOND mode. Nothing has ever
    asked this PLL for one. 154 MHz is exact; a mode whose pixel clock is not
    an integer divisor of any reachable VCO will land on the nearest lower rate
    and nothing in this patch reports how far off it is. The falsifier is a
    modeset at, say, 1280x1024/108 MHz where SRC0_MVID does not compute back to
    the requested clock.

Two flags carry weight and are not decoration:
  CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT on edp-tv. Which of the four sources drives the
    panel is a pipeline decision. Without it ccu_mux_helper_determine_rate()
    walks all four parents and can select one whose PLL is switched off --
    ccu_nm_recalc_rate() reports a rate from the register fields regardless of
    PLL_EN, so a dead PLL_VIDEO0 advertises 1188 MHz. The mux comes from the
    device tree (`arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`, assigned-clock-parents) and never from a rate request.
  CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE on the PLLs, as on every other PLL in this file.

WHY PLL_VIDEO0 AND PLL_VIDEO2 ARE HERE
--------------------------------------
Not for their own sake -- for the mux. ccu_mux_helper_get_parent() returns the
raw register field as a parent index, so an incompletely populated mux does not
fail loudly: it reports the wrong parent, or leaves the clock orphaned when the
index exceeds num_parents. A mux table with one entry does not help either,
because with num_parents == 1 the framework never calls .set_parent and the
field is never written. The honest options were "all four" or "none", and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset`
already took "none".

They are not a guess. The UM documents all three PLL_VIDEOx registers
field-for-field identically (reset 0x40126200 for
each), and the 6 GiB board read all three on the same day with every set bit landing on a
documented field. They cost nothing at runtime: both are off at reset, nothing
enables them, and clk_disable_unused() finds bits that are already clear.
PERI0PLL2X, the fourth input, is the pll-peri0-2x this driver already has.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT
-----------------------------
The 3X taps (bit 26 / [18:16]). No consumer in this tree, so a clock for them
would be dead code. Two consequences, both named rather than discovered later:

  - Register dumps will differ from the by-hand picture by exactly one bit.
    After this driver configures the pixel clock, PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL reads
    0xF9524C00, not the 0xFD524C00 of the by-hand picture above. The
    difference is bit 26 = PLL_OUTPUT1_GATE, which the by-hand write set only
    because it wrote a whole word transcribed from a vendor live capture. The
    UM asks for exactly what we do: with several outputs on a PLL, set the
    output gate of each output actually used.
    We use one. Every bit that carries the rate is identical.
  - Retuning PLL_VIDEO1 still moves VIDEO1PLL3X (792 -> 616 MHz at our
    factors), and the UM's PLL distribution figure puts CSI_MASTER on
    PLL_VIDEO1. Nothing in this tree uses CSI; whoever adds
    it inherits this.

VIDEOPLL_GATE_EN_REG (0x1910) -- the SECOND gate stage, in series with each
PLL's own OUTPUT0/1_GATE. Reset 0x00770077 has every tap enabled and the 6 GiB board read
0x00770000 with the eDP link live, so the reset value is already permissive and
the by-hand session never wrote it. Not modelled, named here so that a driver
which one day clears it knows there is a second gate to reopen.

CM_VO1_CFG_REG (0x1B38) MODULE_MODE. Not written, and that is measured: the 6 GiB board
read the reset default 0x00020000 (STATUS = Power ON, MODULE_MODE = 0) while
its eDP link was up, so MODULE_MODE is not the effective gate for a running VO1
(and the same finding on USB).

