THE ONE OPEN QUESTION THIS PATCH WAS ASKED TO CLOSE -- CLOSED
-------------------------------------------------------------
Earlier in the bring-up the ~200 MHz net feeding the eDP bus gate was called
ahb in our own clock model, which was the thing under test: the rate was
settled, the name was not.

The name is settled, by the UM, and it is AHB. AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG 0x05C0
bit 4 is VID_OUT1_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG, the gate of the Video Out1 AHB clock
-- so the UM itself calls the bus feeding the whole VIDEO_OUT1
domain, which is where EDP_BGR, TCONTV1_BGR and DPSS_TOP1_BGR live, the AHB.
It is one bit away from SERDES_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG at bit 8, which this driver
already models as bus-serdes parented to ahb.

The rate agrees from two directions: UM 4.1.4.3 lists AHB at a typical 200 MHz with the maximum
also 200 MHz, and the 8 GiB board reads AHB_CLK_REG 0x02002500 = 0x03000002 -> source 3 = pll-peri0-600m,
M = /3 -> 200 MHz exactly.

So "ahb" in this driver is no longer an inference for the display gates. It
still is for bus-ths, bus-pwm0/1 and bus-edp, whose comments say so; the UM
names a Video Out1 AHB gate and no THS or PWM one.

WHAT ccu_nm_ops DOES NOT DO, AND WHY IT MATTERS ON A BOARD
----------------------------------------------------------
It does not wait for LOCK on the enable path. ccu_helper_wait_for_lock() runs
at the end of ccu_nm_set_rate() only, so UM 4.1.4.1 Method 2 -- set factors,
PLL_EN, LOCK_ENABLE, wait LOCK, wait 20 us, then open the output gate
(A733 user manual) -- is honoured when the rate is set and not when the clock
is merely enabled. A consumer must therefore clk_set_rate() before relying on
the output, which a modeset does anyway. Not papered over with a private op:
this file uses mainline primitives only, and the pending observable below is
exactly the thing that would catch it.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 as part of the full series against a freshly
unpacked 6.18.36; ccu-sun60iw2.o builds warning-free at ARCH=arm64 W=1;
scripts/check-binding-ids.sh green over all eight A733 headers; checkpatch
--strict reports no new class of finding beyond the three every other CCU
patch in this tree produces. Ids allocated when this was written: clocks
60-68, resets 25-26, next free 69 and 27.

NOT hardware-proven. What would prove it, as one observable, and what would
falsify it: with a consumer holding
these clocks, PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL @0x02002140 reads 0xF9524C00 -- bit 28 LOCK set,
N = 0x4C, P0 field 5, M1 0, and bit 26 clear because we use one output -- and
EDP_TV_CLK_REG @0x02003640 reads 0x81000001. Falsified by those registers
reading correct while SRC0_MVID @0x0574084C does not follow: the CCU echoing a
write proves the write landed, not that a clock came out of it. MVID is
read-only in asynchronous mode and is measured by the DP core from the incoming
video clock, which is why it is the falsifier and the CCU read is not.

